hi there steve here again to talk about  language learning  uh to talk about myths to talk about  introverts and extroverts  um myths why uh because um  there are lots of myths you hear people  say  that you need a special talent to learn  languages that you can't learn past a  certain age  that you need to have a musical ear  and of course i don't think any of those  things are necessarily true or  even if they were true to some extent it  really wouldn't matter and by the way  i'm picking up on a theme that i covered  in a blog post and i'm going to put a  link to the blog post here in the  description box so that any of you who  use these videos for your  english language learning you can then  read  sort of on the same theme at my blog  so you know you can't learn past a  certain age well i don't know if i'm a  proof that that that's not the case but  even if i'm  not as good at learning languages as i  was 30 40 50 years ago even if my  neurons are running a little more slowly  so what that doesn't prevent me from  learning languages it doesn't prevent me  from having the  the pleasure like i spent the day  building a deck listening to farsi not  understanding much of it and enjoying it  all and then i was able to sit down and  go through some of that  and uh  who cares if somebody 50 years younger  is better than me  again this this business about you know  you have to have a musical ear i lived  in japan for nine years the japanese are  fabulous singers uh every time i went to  a you know a karaoke place and i had to  sing i mean  the foreigners were the worst singers  always the japanese are great singers  but that doesn't make them good language  learners necessarily because there's so  much else that goes into language  learning it's got so little to do with  the musical ear  uh  you know  talent maybe some people are better than  others some people are better at golf  than others that doesn't mean that  anyone who wants to can't play golf you  can still enjoy it so none of those  things matter and the same goes for  introvert extrovert you know  uh  some people say you have to be an  extrovert you have to be willing to go  and talk and be outgoing and exuberant  and stuff  and not necessarily because  language learning is about  you know being interested being  motivated believing you can get there  spending a lot of time listening and  reading  engaging people eventually in  you know meaningful conversation  uh spending the time  uh so maybe an extroverted person will  go out there with you know a  limited grasp of the language and just  try out whatever he has or she has and  maybe an introverted person will be a  little more shy a little more careful  but even in our own language introverted  people  don't end up speaking their native  language  more poorly worse than extroverted  people  uh introverted people don't have less to  say  introverted people don't have fewer  interests  so why would introverted people be at  any disadvantage when it comes to  learning a foreign language it doesn't  make any sense to me at all  of course the idea that that you  shouldn't worry about how you sign you  shouldn't feel shy about your language  and that some people can be so hung up  about making mistakes that they're  reluctant to use whatever they have i  mean that's not a good thing  but  someone who's  devoted a lot of time to studying a  language and learning words and reading  in that language and listening in that  language they are acquiring the language  and at whatever time they feel  comfortable they will start using that  language  and there may be some extroverts that  are better than some introverts some  introverts that are better some than  some extroverts it's not a condition for  learning languages so there you have it  a little bit of a discussion of  introvert and extroverts as it applies  to language learning and if you want to  read more on it if you're you know  wanting to work on your english you can  find this  post on my blog thank you for listening  bye for now  [Music]  you
 

